];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Whole Foods Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/whole-foods-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best Whole Foods protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people it is the 365 by Whole Foods Market Whey Protein Isolate, ideally the 2 lb unflavored or vanilla tub. It gives 25 g of protein in about 110 to 120 calories, is listed in the NSF Certified for Sport database, and works out to about $1.65 to $1.76 per 25 g at the listed price. Vegans should pick Garden of Life or Orgain instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Whole Foods 365 protein powder third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The 365 Whey Protein Isolate is. NSF Certified for Sport lists it in chocolate, vanilla and unflavored with specific lot numbers at the time of writing. We found no third-party certification for the 365 whey concentrate, the older grass-fed whey or the plant smoothie powder, so check which tub you are buying before you rely on the seal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in 365 whey protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The 365 Whey Protein Isolate gives 25 g per scoop, with a 30 g scoop for unflavored, 32 g for vanilla and 33 g for chocolate. The 365 Whey Concentrate gives 20 g per serving. The older 365 grass-fed whey gave 20 g in a 27.5 g scoop. Figures are from Whole Foods and NSF listings and can vary by flavor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Whole Foods protein powder have artificial sweeteners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Whole Foods says its supplement standards ban sucralose, saccharin and aspartame, among more than 150 ingredients. Older 365 whey tubs were sweetened with stevia and, in the grass-fed vanilla, monk fruit. The current isolate listings do not print the ingredient panel online, so read the tub if you avoid stevia specifically."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 365 whey protein good for lactose intolerance?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The isolate is the better 365 choice, because filtering removes most of the lactose that a concentrate keeps. It is not certified lactose-free, so very sensitive people may still react; start with a half scoop. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid all whey. Ask a doctor or dietitian if you have a diagnosed condition."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Whole Foods sell vegan protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. The 365 Protein Plantpowder Smoothie gives 15 g of pea, sacha inchi and hemp protein per scoop, and a Whole Foods Market Plant Protein and Greens powder lists 20 g, though stock is patchy. National brands on the Whole Foods site include Garden of Life Raw Organic Meal, Orgain Organic Vegan and Vega All-in-One."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Whole Foods protein powder cheaper than Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes. Whole Foods listed the 2 lb 365 isolate at $49.99 when we checked, and Amazon prices for the same tub have moved above and below that. Prime members get extra Whole Foods sale prices. Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not tub price, and buy from listings sold by Amazon or Whole Foods directly."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Store Brands",
    tags: [
      "whole foods protein powder",
      "365 whey protein isolate",
      "whole foods market protein powder",
      "365 by whole foods market protein powder",
      "whole foods vegan protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Cheap Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-cheap-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Whole Foods Protein Powder</Fragment>
      What Whole Foods Market actually stocks under its 365 house brand and from national brands, what the labels say, and which tub is worth the money per gram of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-whole-foods-sells">What Whole Foods Sells</a>
      <a href="#365-isolate">365 Whey Isolate Label</a>
      <a href="#365-concentrate">365 Concentrate and Older Tubs</a>
      <a href="#vegan">Vegan Options</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#third-party">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#name-brands">Name Brands at Whole Foods</a>
      <a href="#vs-stores">vs Other Stores</a>
      <a href="#amazon">On Amazon?</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best Whole Foods protein powder for most shoppers is the 365 by Whole Foods Market Whey Protein Isolate: 25 g of protein in a 30 to 33 g scoop, about 110 to 120 calories, and listed in the NSF Certified for Sport database in all three flavors. Store prices vary, so the value case depends on your branch. Below: every 365 powder we could verify, the plant options, cost per 25 g and the national brands on the shelf.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Whole Foods Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whole Foods protein powder mostly means the 365 by Whole Foods Market whey line. The newest 365 Whey Protein Isolate gives 25 g of protein per scoop in vanilla, chocolate and unflavored, and all three appear in the NSF Certified for Sport database. Whole Foods' online listings showed $49.99 for the 2 lb tub and $32.99 for the 1 lb tub when we checked in November 2026, which works out to roughly $1.65 to $2.40 per 25 g of protein depending on size and flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a certified whey isolate without leaving the grocery store, or you are lactose-sensitive and want an isolate rather than a concentrate. <strong>Skip it if</strong> lowest cost is the goal: warehouse-club whey runs well under $1 per 25 g, and our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheap protein powder rankings</a> list the better-value tubs. And if you searched this because you want a powder made from whole foods rather than a powder sold at Whole Foods, our <a href="/best-whole-food-protein-powder/">whole-food protein powder guide</a> is the page you want.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Whole Foods powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Size and listed price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">365 Whey Protein Isolate, Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$49.99 / 2 lb (907 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g (32 g scoop)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.76</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">365 Whey Protein Isolate, Unflavored</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$49.99 / 2 lb (907 g)*</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g (30 g scoop)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.65*</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">365 Whey Protein Isolate, Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$32.99 / 1 lb (454 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g (33 g scoop)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.40</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">365 Whey Concentrate, Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Price not shown online</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check your store</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            *We saw the $49.99 price on the 2 lb isolate listings; confirm the unflavored price at your store. Serving counts are worked out from the package weights and scoop sizes in the NSF listings. Whole Foods sets prices by store and region, and Prime members get extra sale prices, so your shelf tag can differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Whole Foods Protein Powders Worth Considering
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first three cards are 365 by Whole Foods Market powders, the house brand Whole Foods sells in stores, through its website and on Amazon. The last two are national-brand plant powders that Whole Foods lists on its own site, for shoppers who want a dairy-free tub. Nutrition comes from the Whole Foods listings and the NSF Certified for Sport database at the time of writing; where a price could not be confirmed, the card says so rather than guessing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-whole-foods-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Protein Powders Does Whole Foods Sell?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whole Foods sells two kinds of protein powder: its own 365 by Whole Foods Market tubs and a curated set of national brands, most of them organic or plant-based. On the Whole Foods website the 365 range currently centres on two whey lines. The first is 365 Whey Protein Isolate in vanilla, chocolate and unflavored, sold in 1 lb and 2 lb tubs. The second is 365 Whey Concentrate Protein Powder in the same three flavors, sold in a 32 oz tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older 365 listings are still live on the site and on Amazon, which confuses a lot of shoppers. These include a grass-fed whey protein in chocolate and vanilla (11.6 oz, 18 oz and 19.4 oz tubs), an earlier grass-fed whey isolate in 15 to 17 oz tubs, and a 12 oz Whole Foods Market grass-fed whey from before the 365 rebrand. Several of these show no price or are marked unavailable, so treat them as legacy products and check the shelf before you plan around one.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the plant side, the 365 range is thin. Whole Foods lists a 365 Protein Plantpowder Smoothie in coconut almond and a Whole Foods Market Plant Protein and Greens powder in French vanilla and rich chocolate. The French vanilla listing we saw said it was only available for shipping to customers in California and that its packaging was moving from the Whole Foods Market label to 365, so availability is patchy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The national brands fill the gaps. Whole Foods product pages we could open include Garden of Life Raw Organic Meal, Orgain Organic Vegan Protein, Vega All-in-One and Orgain's sport line, and its supplement promotions feature collagen from Vital Proteins and Ancient Nutrition. What you will not usually find is the big bodybuilding whey brands in 5 lb tubs. Whole Foods says it bans more than 150 ingredients from the supplements it sells, including hydrogenated oils, high-fructose corn syrup, sucralose, saccharin and aspartame, which rules out many mainstream gym powders.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Stock Note</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The website and your store will not match exactly.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Whole Foods shows prices only after you pick a store, and each region sets its own range. Everything here reflects the Whole Foods and Amazon listings at the time of writing. If a 365 tub is missing from your shelf, it is often still orderable for delivery or pickup.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="365-isolate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in 365 Whey Protein Isolate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            365 Whey Protein Isolate gives 25 g of protein per scoop, with about 120 calories in vanilla and chocolate and about 110 in unflavored, per the NSF Certified for Sport listings at the time of writing. The vanilla scoop is 32 g with 2 g of carbohydrate; the chocolate scoop is 33 g with 3 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of fat; the unflavored scoop is 30 g. Those figures make it a lean isolate, roughly 76 to 83% protein by weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole Foods' listings add that each serving supplies 5 g of BCAAs and that the whey comes from milk from cows not treated with rBST. The NSF entries tag the product as free of GMOs. That is the full set of claims we could confirm. The current listings do not print the ingredient panel online, so we could not confirm which sweetener the flavored tubs use; the older 365 isolate and grass-fed tubs used stevia, and the grass-fed vanilla also listed monk fruit. Because Whole Foods bans sucralose and aspartame from its supplements, you will not find them here.
          </p>

          <p>
            An isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat that a concentrate keeps. That makes it the better 365 choice for people who get bloated on regular whey, though it is not lactose-free in the strict sense, and anyone with a milk allergy should avoid it entirely. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate rankings</a> compare it with the national isolates if you want a second opinion on value.
          </p>

          <p>
            The unflavored version is the most useful tub in the range for cooks. With nothing added to fight against, it disappears into oatmeal, pancake batter, coffee and smoothies, and its 30 g scoop has the highest protein density of the three. If you prefer to drink a shake with water, the flavored tubs make more sense; just note that the chocolate 1 lb tub is the most expensive way to buy the isolate per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The previous 365 isolate, still visible in some listings as Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate in a 16.9 oz tub, gave the same 25 g of protein in a 32 g scoop with 120 calories, 0.5 g of fat and 1 g of sugar. If you have one in the pantry, the nutrition is near-identical; the main difference is that we found no NSF listing for that older version.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="365-concentrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What About 365 Whey Concentrate and the Older Grass-Fed Tubs?
          </h2>

          <p>
            365 Whey Concentrate Protein Powder gives 20 g of protein and 4 g of BCAAs per serving, per the Whole Foods listing, in a 32 oz tub of vanilla, chocolate or unflavored. It uses milk from cows not treated with rBST. The online listings did not show calories, the ingredient panel or a price, so we cannot give a cost per 25 g for it; check the shelf tag and do the arithmetic in the cost section below before you assume it is the bargain option.
          </p>

          <p>
            A concentrate keeps more of milk's lactose, fat and carbohydrate than an isolate, which is why the protein per scoop is lower. That is not a flaw in itself. Many people digest concentrate perfectly well, and it often tastes a little creamier. But the 365 concentrate is not listed in the NSF Certified for Sport database the way the isolate is, so if testing is the reason you are choosing a store brand, the isolate is the one to pick.
          </p>

          <p>
            The older 365 Grass-Fed Whey Protein Powder is a different product again. Its chocolate listing shows a 27.5 g scoop with 110 calories, 20 g of protein, 2.5 g of fat, 2 g of sugar and 280 mg of sodium, with 20 servings in the 19.4 oz tub and 12 in the 11.6 oz tub; the smaller tub was labeled Non-GMO Project Verified. The vanilla version listed stevia, monk fruit, xanthan gum and natural flavors alongside the whey. If grass-fed sourcing matters to you, our <a href="/best-grass-fed-whey-protein/">grass-fed whey guide</a> lists brands that still state it on current labels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our practical advice: buy the isolate if your store carries it, buy the concentrate only if the shelf price beats the isolate per 25 g, and do not go hunting for the legacy grass-fed tubs. The listings for them are older, several are marked out of stock, and you cannot easily tell how long a given tub has been sitting on a shelf.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vegan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does Whole Foods Have a Vegan Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, but the house-brand vegan options are small and patchy, so most Whole Foods vegan protein comes from national brands. The 365 Protein Plantpowder Smoothie in coconut almond gives 15 g of protein in a 22 g scoop with 80 calories, 14 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and 200 mg of sodium, per the Whole Foods listing. The protein is a blend of pea, sacha inchi and organic hemp, with a small greens blend, stevia and papain. It is Non-GMO Project Verified and comes in a 9 oz tub of about 12 servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a light smoothie booster rather than a full protein powder; 15 g per scoop means you need almost two scoops for a 25 g dose. The Whole Foods Market Plant Protein and Greens in French vanilla is listed at 20 g of vegan protein and 120 calories per serving, which is closer to a normal shake, but the listing we saw restricted shipping to California and flagged a packaging change, so it may not be on your shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a dependable dairy-free tub at Whole Foods, the national brands are the better bet. Garden of Life Raw Organic Meal gives 20 g of pea-led protein, 7 g of fiber and 0 g of sugar in 150 calories and carries USDA Organic, NSF Contents Certified and NSF Certified Gluten-Free seals, per its Whole Foods listing. Orgain Organic Vegan 21 g gives 21 g of protein in 160 calories with 7 g of fiber and is USDA Organic. Vega All-in-One gives 20 g in 150 calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of those plant tubs is NSF Certified for Sport, and plant proteins as a group tend to carry more measurable heavy metals than whey, because peas and rice take up minerals from soil. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein powder rankings</a> and the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">heavy-metals guide</a> cover which brands publish their contaminant testing.
          </p>
        </div>

