        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Whole Foods Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, the 365 Whey Protein Isolate is: NSF Certified for Sport lists Whole Foods Market Whey Protein Isolate in chocolate, vanilla and unflavored, with lot numbers, in its public database at the time of writing. That is unusual for a grocery store brand. Neither Sprouts nor Trader Joe's publishes a sport certification for its own powders, and most supermarket protein carries no third-party seal at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport means an independent lab has checked that the product contains what the label says, screened it for hundreds of substances banned in sport, and audited the factory, with ongoing lot testing. It is the certification drug-tested athletes are told to look for. It is mainly a banned-substance and label-accuracy check rather than a promise of the lowest possible heavy metals; for metals specifically, our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder guide</a> explains what the seal does and does not cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other 365 powders are a different story. We found no NSF or Informed Choice listing for the 365 whey concentrate, the older grass-fed whey or the plant smoothie powder. Whole Foods does apply its own supplement quality standards, including its banned-ingredient list, but that is a retailer policy rather than independent product testing, and the brand does not publish lab results for those tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always match the lot number on your tub to the NSF database if certification matters to you, because a certified product name can sit next to an older, uncertified formula on the same shelf. The NSF listings for the 365 isolate show several lot numbers per flavor, and the 2 lb unflavored and vanilla and the 1 lb chocolate are the package sizes listed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="name-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which National Brands Are Worth Buying at Whole Foods?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garden of Life is the national brand most worth buying at Whole Foods, because it is the one with the strongest certifications on the shelf. Its Raw Organic Meal gives 20 g of protein with 7 g of fiber and probiotics in a 150-calorie scoop, which suits people using a shake as a light breakfast. Our <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life review</a> covers the rest of its range, including the NSF-certified Sport line, which we did not find on the Whole Foods site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Orgain is the most common plant brand across grocery chains, and Whole Foods lists its Organic Vegan 21 g powder and its sport line. Orgain is USDA Organic and widely liked for taste, but per gram of protein it is usually pricier than whey, and it carries no sport certification. Our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain protein powder review</a> goes through the labels and sweeteners in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vega All-in-One is a meal-style plant shake with greens and probiotics; 20 g of protein in 150 calories is fine, but the small 13.2 oz tub holds only nine servings, so check the price per serving carefully. Collagen from Vital Proteins and Ancient Nutrition also features in Whole Foods supplement promotions, but collagen is not a complete protein and should not be counted the same as whey; our <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder explainer</a> covers why.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our view: for whey, the 365 isolate beats every other whey you are likely to find at Whole Foods on both certification and price per gram. For plant protein, pay for a national brand, because the house range is too thin to rely on.
          </p>
        </div>

