            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing Whole Foods with whole-food protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Searching for Whole Foods protein powder and buying a whole-food blend, or the reverse, is common. The 365 isolate is a filtered whey, not a whole-food product. If you want minimally processed, food-based powders, use a whole-food guide instead of the store aisle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Grabbing the legacy grass-fed tub by mistake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Older 365 Grass-Fed Whey and Grass-Fed Whey Isolate tubs still appear online and sometimes on shelves. They give 20 to 25 g of protein but are not in the NSF Certified for Sport database. Look for the plain Whey Protein Isolate label and match the lot number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 1 lb chocolate tub as your default</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 1 lb chocolate isolate works out to about $2.40 per 25 g, roughly 45% more than the 2 lb unflavored tub. Buy the small tub to test a flavor, then move to the 2 lb size once you know you will finish it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting the plant smoothie powder as a full serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 365 coconut almond Plantpowder gives 15 g of protein per scoop. Treating one scoop as your post-workout dose leaves you around 10 g short of the 25 g most research uses. Use two scoops, or a national-brand vegan powder with 20 g or more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the store ban list means tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whole Foods bans more than 150 ingredients from its supplements, which keeps out sucralose and aspartame. That is a retailer policy, not independent testing. Only the 365 isolate carries a third-party sport certification among the house-brand powders.
              </p>
